{-
   The negative row for §6.5's allocation sinking - `sinkAllocations` in
   `resolve_ssa/js/opt_alias.cpp`.

   An escaping local is a `Value` place on the JavaScript target, so the storage *is* the host
   object and the conversion builds one where the frame slot is declared, which is the entry block.
   Sinking that allocation to the block that dominates its uses is what stops a path leaving the
   function from paying for it - and the one destination it may never take is **inside a loop the
   definition was not in**, because the object is then rebuilt once per iteration and the local is
   re-zeroed with it.

   `tally` is that program: every use of it is inside the `for`, so the lowest common ancestor of
   its uses is the loop body and a rule that does not ask about loops moves it there. The answer
   says which happened - the 63 below is three iterations accumulating into one record, and a
   record rebuilt every iteration answers 31.

   `held` is the positive half in the same body, so that the fixture is not only a refusal: its
   uses are all below the early return, and it is the shape `normalizeForm` in the library's own
   Unicode cases has - five allocations on a path that returns before touching any of them.

   There is deliberately **no** case here for the third condition - that a `Clone` is not one of
   the kinds that may move. It is a real refusal (what a duplicate copies is whatever its source
   holds when it runs, so one carried below a write to that source copies the written value) and no
   program in this corpus distinguishes it: a duplicate's result is stored into its own local's
   slot in the block that made it, so the "only across a block boundary" rule refuses it before the
   kind is looked at. Allowing it moves 19 bytes of `Base.js` and changes no answer anywhere.

   Two fields rather than three in both records, because a three-field record of primitives is
   `Parts` on this target (J74's scalarization band) and there would be no allocation to sink.
-}

data Tally {total: Int, seen: Int}

data Note {tag: Int, count: Int}

@noinline fn bump(&t: Tally, by: Int):
    t.total = t.total + by
    t.seen = t.seen + 1

@noinline fn note(&n: Note, by: Int):
    n.tag = n.tag + by
    n.count = n.count + 1

@noinline fn early(n: Int) = n < 0

fn main() -> Int:
    let &tally = Tally {total: 0, seen: 0}
    let &last = 0 :: Int

    for i in 1..4:
        bump(tally, i)
        last = tally.total * 10 + tally.seen

    let &held = Note {tag: 0, count: 0}
    if early(last) then return -1

    note(held, 5)
    note(held, 7)

    return last + held.tag + held.count
